## Interface: 20506
## Title: WarriorKit
## Notes: Ctrl-click raid marking, casting on what the mouse is over with a key you bound by dragging the spell into a slot, a Charge button that aims by camera out of combat, a character sheet of the addon's own with your gear, your skills, your standings and how often you miss on it, a talent window with all three trees side by side and nothing to scroll and what unlearning them would cost along the bottom, a spell book with one row per spell and every rank you know folded behind a button beside it, a warrior bar loadout, threat-coloured enemy bars, damage healing and threat meters, a nag for the sharpening stone and the racial you forgot, two rows of cooldowns, a shaman's four totem slots with a hole where one is missing, the seconds a fight is made of over the minutes, square class-coloured player and target frames, party and raid blocks ordered by role, your own cast bar, a square minimap with the addon buttons collected behind one square, a chat window of the addon's own with a room per conversation in place of the client's, groups for the people who matter and a voice channel joined at login, a loot stream and a combat log drawn as scrolling feeds with the addon's own tooltips on them, the addon's own tooltip on a creature you point at in the world with where you stand on it under the name, a mail window that colours a recipient green for one of yours, blue for somebody you know and red for a stranger and splits more than twelve attachments across as many mails as it takes, a quest log three columns wide with every quest you are on down the left grouped by zone, what this one wants in the middle and what it pays on the right, a dungeon log the same three columns wide with every boss in the game down the left in level order, the dungeon's own map in the middle with the ones you have looted marked on it and what this one drops on the right, one window for your bags with what you are carrying sorted into piles and how many slots are free counted along the bottom, a filter for the red text in the middle of the screen, fast looting, trash selling, repairs paid for you, a whispered thank you to a stranger who buffs you, five seconds of the eighties every time you level, a longer camera, a carried Edit Mode layout and stripped bar art.
## Author: baudin
## IconTexture: Interface\AddOns\WarriorKit\Media\Icon.tga
## Version: 1.9
## SavedVariables: WarriorKitDB
## SavedVariablesPerCharacter: WarriorKitCharDB
## X-Category: Combat

# The fallback TOC, which is what the TBC Anniversary client (2.5.6, interface
# 20506) loads. WarriorKit_Vanilla.toc is the same file list for Classic Era.
# check.sh fails if the two lists ever drift apart.

# Core first. It creates ns.db, the API shims and the registry every feature
# signs into, and the panel walks that registry at PLAYER_LOGIN.
Core\Core.lua

# Where a Blizzard frame goes when this addon draws it instead: one frame,
# hidden at birth, that nothing can show. A frame re-parented into it is not
# drawn whatever the client calls on it, which is the guarantee ns.Strip in
# Core.lua cannot make on its own, because SetShown is resolved in C and never
# reads the Lua Show it replaced. Right after Core.lua, which owns ns.Strip and
# ns.Blocked, and above every part that takes one of the client's frames down.
Core\Attic.lua

# What the addon knows about a class, and one file per class that knows it.
# Class.lua is the registry and the question "which class is this"; Spec.lua is
# the question "which half of it are you playing", answered off a signature
# talent and then off your trees; each file beside them is the whole of what the
# addon knows about one class, and nothing outside that file names one of its
# spells. A class nobody has written a file
# for gets everything below that never asks. Loads before Core\Stance.lua and
# before every part, because all of them read facts off it and none of them
# reads one at load.
Class\Class.lua
Class\Spec.lua
Class\Warrior.lua
Class\Mage.lua
Class\Shaman.lua
Class\Priest.lua

# Seven shared files that own no setting and draw nothing, the same shape
# ns.ItemInfo has. Gear is what the client will let into each hand and which
# number that hand is; Stance reads your class's own form list off the registry
# above and hands back their localised names and which one you are standing in.
# Piles is which class the client files an item under, what that class is called
# in your language and what order the piles are drawn in, which is what the bag
# window and the merchant window both sort by. Loot turns the server's own loot
# sentences into who got what and how many, off the format strings the client
# used to write them, so a German client is read by German rules; the loot feed
# draws every line and the bag session records your own. QuestItems is which
# row of your log a quest item belongs to, read off Questie's item rows and the
# client's own index, which the bag window's quest lanes and the clutter list
# both turn on. CombatLog is the one call that hands over a combat log line,
# made once per line and handed to whichever parts are reading; six of them made
# it themselves, three for a feature that was switched off, and the client event
# is unregistered while nobody is subscribed. Need is the one question a loot
# row, a bag square and the chip over both of them ask about an item: why it
# matters to you, answered off the quest log, the reagent list and the loot
# filter in that order, so that two of them cannot rank the same item
# differently. Every one of them was a part's private knowledge until a second
# part needed the same answer, and all seven load before anything that asks.
Core\Gear.lua
Core\Stance.lua
Core\Piles.lua
Core\QuestItems.lua
Core\Loot.lua
Core\CombatLog.lua

# Where you are standing, asked once and held until you walk out of it: the map
# id, what this client calls that map, whether it is an instance, and the area
# id Questie's database keys on. Down here rather than in Quests because the
# quest tracker and the map window both want it and neither may name the
# other's files. Reads UI\Chart.lua and Core\Core.lua's Questie door, both
# inside its own functions, so it loads anywhere above the parts that ask.
Core\Here.lua

# The shared unit layer. What a mob or a group member is, asked once: the
# palette, the level tag, who is in the group, what they have spent their points
# on, which role that makes them and what the threat API says. Between Core and
# UI because none of it draws anything and everything that draws a unit wants
# it. Order inside the folder is dependency order: Color before Level, Roster
# before Threat, and Roster then Spec then Role, because Role asks the first two
# which unit a GUID is and which tree that unit took.
#
# Spec.lua was Meter\Spec.lua. It reads talent trees for you and for anyone the
# client will let you inspect, which is the only source of a real role on a
# client that has none, and a part may not name a file outside its own tree.
Unit\Unit.lua
Unit\Color.lua
Unit\Level.lua
Unit\Roster.lua
Unit\Spec.lua
Unit\Role.lua
Unit\Threat.lua

# The drawing layer, between Core and everything that draws. It owns the pixel
# grid, so it loads before the panel and before any part that puts a frame up.
UI\Pixel.lua

# One OnUpdate, registered rather than written out. Every throttled tick in the
# addon comes through here, which is what lets scripts/hot.lua derive the set of
# functions a frame handler reaches instead of check.sh carrying that closure by
# hand. Loads with the pixel grid because it is the same layer and because a
# part that draws may want a tick before anything else it uses exists.
UI\Ticker.lua
# The animation library, which is its own thing rather than part of the drawing
# layer. Ck\Animations.lua is a tween engine: a frame, a duration, an easing and
# up to two channels, advanced on one tick that stops itself when nothing is
# moving. Ck\Float.lua is the first thing built on it, a column of messages that
# slide in from an edge, hold and fade.
#
# Everything under Ck\ is written to know nothing about WarriorKit, so lifting
# it into an addon of its own is a change to one line and to these two lists.
# After UI\Ticker.lua, which is the tick it runs on, and after UI\Pixel.lua,
# whose UI.Whole is what snaps a travelling coordinate to a whole pixel.
Ck\Animations.lua
Ck\Float.lua
# The second thing built on the animation layer, and a different object rather
# than a setting on the first. A lane re-aims a message that is already
# travelling, because the one above it expired; nothing in a stream is ever
# re-aimed, so a number's place is a function of how long it has been alive and
# the arc and the scale envelope are evaluated rather than interpolated between
# two stored ends. After Ck\Animations.lua, whose easing curves it shares and
# which is the one thing the two have in common.
Ck\Stream.lua
UI\Draw.lua

# The gauge, between the drawing primitives and anything that puts a bar up. A
# status bar with a flat fill and the spent part of it behind, which the enemy
# bars and the skinned unit frames both draw and both used to draw their own
# way. After UI\Draw.lua because it builds its underlay out of ns.Fill, and
# after Unit\Color.lua because the spent part is a fifth of the fill's colour
# and that fraction belongs to the unit palette.
UI\Gauge.lua

UI\Text.lua

# One aura, drawn: the cropped icon, the hairline round it, the sweep over the
# art, the time left and the stack count, and whether it is yours. The client's
# Cooldown frame draws the wedge, reversed, so an aura fills as it runs out
# where a cooldown empties. The debuff row on an enemy bar
# and the row under the skinned target block are both made of these. After
# UI\Draw.lua for the cropped icon and UI\Text.lua for the shared fonts, and
# after Unit\Color.lua, because what a debuff of yours looks like belongs to
# the unit palette and not to either row.
UI\Aura.lua

UI\Flow.lua

# One ability, drawn: the icon, the cooldown swipe, the timer, the count and
# the border that carries the status. After UI\Draw.lua for the cropped icon
# and UI\Text.lua for the shared fonts, and before every part that puts a
# castable square on the screen.
UI\Ability.lua

# The widget library, on top of that grid. Theme is the palette and the pixel
# metrics, Stack lays a column out and asks every row how tall it is, Scroll
# clips and scrolls, Widgets is the kit a page is built from, Placeable is the
# dragging, and Window is the chrome with its rail and its tab strip. All six
# load before Core\Panel.lua, which is the first thing that builds a page out
# of them.
UI\Theme.lua

# The item square, and the pile headings that go over a column of them. After
# UI\Theme.lua, whose palette says what an item's grade is drawn in, and after
# UI\Draw.lua and UI\Text.lua, which are the icon and the count on it. The bag
# window and the merchant window are the two that draw one today; a bank, a loot
# window and an auction house are the same square again.
UI\Slot.lua

UI\Stack.lua
UI\Scroll.lua

# A column of lines that grows from the bottom and scrolls, which is what a
# chat window is made of. After UI\Scroll.lua because it borrows that file's
# bar, and after UI\Theme.lua because it takes the palette into file-scope
# locals as it loads, the same as everything else on this layer.
UI\Log.lua

# The tooltip, in four files. Every hover in the addon went to GameTooltip
# until this existed, which meant a parchment scroll with a gold border rising
# out of an interface that has neither anywhere else.
#
# Scan.lua is the only file in the addon that names GameTooltip. It points a
# hidden one at an item, an action slot, an aura or a unit and reads the font
# strings back, because the stats, the rank and the cost are computed inside the
# game and no API hands them over. It is also where the client's own tooltip is
# held down while ours is up, which is the world hover's half of the same rule:
# one file talks to Blizzard's box, whichever direction it is talking in.
#
# Tooltip.lua is the box: the theme's palette, the shared font, the pixel grid,
# and a list of lines drawn on it.
#
# Tip.lua is what goes in the box. A hover names a subject, this works out what
# the whole addon has to say about it, and a part hooks a line of its own in by
# calling ns.Tip.Source once at load. It loads after the box because it calls
# it, and before every part because every part registers into it.
#
# Fresh.lua keeps an open box true. A tooltip here was built once, at the moment
# the pointer arrived, and never again: the threat percentage on a mob you held
# through a whole pull was the figure it had been on the frame you got there.
# This reads a stamp a few times a second, one cheap number per thing in the box
# that can move, and builds the box again on the tick a stamp differs and on no
# other tick. It is a file of its own rather than a tick inside Tip.lua because
# a rebuild has to be marked cold and Tip.lua's one cold marker is already
# spoken for by the settle.
#
# All four after UI\Theme.lua for the palette and UI\Text.lua for the fonts,
# and before every part that answers the mouse. Fresh.lua last of the four: it
# is the only one that calls the other three rather than being called by them.
UI\Scan.lua
UI\Tooltip.lua
UI\Tip.lua
UI\Fresh.lua

# A column of things that happened, newest at the top, older below it, scrolled
# by the wheel. It is not UI\Log.lua, which holds strings and cannot carry an
# icon per line, and it is not UI\Stack.lua, which relays the whole column every
# time a row arrives. After UI\Scroll.lua because it borrows that file's bar,
# and after UI\Tooltip.lua because a row's whole answer to the mouse is one.
UI\Feed.lua

# A zone of the client's own map art, drawn at whatever size the caller gives
# it, with a list of points on top and a wheel that zooms at the cursor. It was
# Quests\Chart.lua and is in the kit because it has two callers: the map behind
# the quest log's middle column and the world map, and it knows nothing about
# either. After UI\Tip.lua, because a dot's whole answer to the mouse is one.
UI\Chart.lua

UI\Widgets.lua

# The room the addon's own rows over the world go into while a screen window is
# up. Nothing but a hidden parent, so it needs no other file and could load
# anywhere; it is here because UI\Window.lua is what shuts it and the seven
# parts that go into it are all built after both.
UI\Hush.lua

# The frame you can unlock and drag, which is twelve parts of this addon and was
# twelve copies of the same block. Placing and chrome are two things: this file
# owns the first, UI\Window.lua owns the second and is a caller of this one.
# After UI\Theme.lua for the rim it draws while a frame is being placed and
# after UI\Text.lua for the name above it, and before every part that puts a
# movable rectangle on the world.
UI\Placeable.lua
UI\Window.lua

# The question a part puts up before it does something that cannot be undone.
# One window for all of them, after UI\Window.lua because it is one of those and
# after UI\Widgets.lua because its two buttons are.
UI\Ask.lua

# The number a part asks for before it does something to that many of a thing:
# how many stacks to buy off a vendor, how many to split off a pile. One window
# for all of them, after UI\Window.lua and UI\Widgets.lua because it is one of
# those and its controls are theirs, and after UI\Slot.lua because it draws the
# thing being counted on that file's square.
UI\Amount.lua

# Which of the client's own frames are on the screen: one switch each, the list
# every part signs into, and the once-a-second pass that puts back anything
# that moved. In Core because it is asked by nine trees and is a fact about none
# of them. It lived in UnitFrames\\Blizzard.lua, which is where it was written
# because unit frames wanted it first, and every other tree then had to name
# UnitFrames to hide a frame that had nothing to do with unit frames.
# After Core\\Attic.lua, which is where a frame it takes down actually goes, and
# after UI\\Ticker.lua for the pass it arms.
Core\BlizzHide.lua

Core\Command.lua
Core\Panel.lua

# The client's own menu: one button of ours at the foot of it, and the whole
# frame drawn in the addon's look. After Core\Panel.lua because that is the
# file that owns the window the button opens, and before every part because it
# belongs to the addon rather than to any of them. The paint loads first
# because Core\Menu.lua hands it the menu and the button on the way past.
Core\MenuSkin.lua
Core\Menu.lua

# The parts. Within each, behaviour first and Feature.lua last, because
# Feature.lua is the only file that names anything outside its own folder.
# Perf first among the parts, because Charge/Icon.lua and the three other
# tickers call ns.Perf.Start by name. They resolve it at tick time rather than
# at load, so the order is belt and braces, and belt and braces is what a nil
# namespace on a ticker is worth: that is 1,189 errors in two minutes.
Perf\Perf.lua
# The frame trace, in the order it is read in. Census counts events, Cause turns
# a frame into a reason, Trace is the tick that records every frame, Hud is the
# window Ctrl-R opens and Key is the key that opens it. Each names only the ones
# above it, so the load order is also the dependency order.
Perf\Census.lua
Perf\Cause.lua
Perf\Trace.lua
Perf\Hud.lua
Perf\Key.lua
Perf\Feature.lua

# The console next: a box that runs a line of Lua and shows what it printed.
# No ticker, and it reads no other part, so it is up whatever else is not.
Console\Console.lua
Console\Feature.lua

Marking\Marking.lua
Marking\Keys.lua
Marking\Feature.lua

# Casting on what the mouse is over, which is this addon's own Clique. Hover.lua
# is the model, Cast.lua is the secure button every key presses, Sheet.lua draws
# the list of what you bound over the world, Panel.lua is the page, and
# Feature.lua last as everywhere else. Cast and Sheet both read Hover, and
# nothing reads either of them but the page.
#
# After Marking, which is the other part built on a modified key and the thing
# under the cursor, and which is where the whole of the override binding
# reasoning is written down. It needs UI\Widgets.lua for the slot you drop a
# spell on and the pixel grid for the list, both of which are far above this
# line.
Hover\Hover.lua
Hover\Cast.lua
Hover\Sheet.lua
Hover\Panel.lua
Hover\Feature.lua

Charge\Charge.lua
Charge\Icon.lua
Charge\Marker.lua
Charge\SoftTarget.lua
Charge\Feature.lua

Targeting\Switch.lua
Targeting\Feature.lua

# Bars you made yourself, each on a key. AdHoc.lua is the list, Bars.lua the
# secure frames, the keys and the tick, Panel.lua the page you design one on,
# and Feature.lua last as everywhere else. It reads Hover\Hover.lua for what
# the cursor is carrying and Buttons\Castable.lua on its tick, both far above.
AdHoc\AdHoc.lua
AdHoc\Bars.lua
AdHoc\Panel.lua
AdHoc\Feature.lua

# Which of the two reactive abilities a slot is holding, and whether its window
# is open. Out of the combat log, warrior only, and read by Slot.lua on the
# bar's ticker, so it loads before it.
Buttons\Reaction.lua

# The other half of the same idea: what the fight has to have done before a
# press lands, for the abilities the client has no opinion about. Read off the
# target rather than out of the log, class data like the windows next door, and
# read by Slot.lua on the same tick, so it loads before it too.
Buttons\Requires.lua

# The ladder for a spell rather than a slot: the two rungs above and the
# client's own refusal, memoised per frame. Slot.lua asks it for the rungs the
# client cannot answer, and the cooldown row and the racial ask it for the
# whole ladder, which is what stops a row saying "ready" over a press that
# does nothing.
Buttons\Castable.lua
Buttons\Slot.lua
Buttons\Layout.lua
Buttons\Ranks.lua

# The bars themselves, after the three files they read and before Feature.lua
# as everywhere else. Slot.lua says what one action slot is doing, Layout.lua
# resolves which slots a bar drives, and Bars.lua is what draws them, so it
# loads last of the three. It also needs UI\Ability.lua, UI\Flow.lua and the
# pixel grid, all of which are far above this line.
# Hiding the client's own action buttons, and putting them back. Its own file
# because it is the one part of the bars that touches a frame this addon does
# not own, and because how you hide a secure button without taking its taint
# with you is a subject rather than a detail. Before Bars.lua, which calls it.
Buttons\Blizzard.lua

# The plan of which bars this client can have, and the answer to which of them
# you want cloned. Before Bars.lua, which reads both at build time. Its own file
# because the answer stopped being a reading of the client the moment there was
# a tick box per bar, and because the plan is the one part of the bars that is
# source code rather than a setting.
Buttons\Which.lua

# What one bar looks like and when it is on the screen: the rows the twelve
# fold into, the colour and the opacity of the ground under them, and whether
# the bar goes down in combat or waits on a key. After Which.lua, whose plan
# holds the default shape of every bar, and before Bars.lua, which asks this
# file what to draw on every layout. Its own file because none of it knows what
# an action slot is and because two of the five answers are a state driver
# rather than a setting the addon can act on itself.
Buttons\Look.lua
Buttons\Bars.lua

# The trace, before the squares that print through it. Read only and off unless
# asked for: what the client says is under the cursor, and every gesture a
# square gets, for the drops on bar 1 that two fixes written from the code have
# now failed to explain.
Buttons\Trace.lua

# What one square answers to the mouse: the tooltip you get by hovering it and
# the drag that fills it. Split out of Bars.lua at the 800 line gate, and a real
# seam rather than a convenient one: everything in it hangs a script on a button
# and touches the cursor, and none of it knows what a bar is. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and after Layout.lua, whose probe it asks before it
# picks anything up.
Buttons\Square.lua

# Which key presses a thing, on the tooltip of the thing. After Square.lua,
# because the hover it adds a line to is that file's, and after Which.lua and
# Bars.lua, whose plan and whose recorded keys it reads the answer off. A source
# on UI\Tip.lua rather than a hover of its own, so the hearthstone in the bag
# window and the same hearthstone on a square say the same key.
Buttons\Bound.lua

# Where each cloned bar sits and the handle you drag it by. After Bars.lua,
# which is the only caller, and separate from it because the bar's shape is
# decided by the plan in source and its position is the one thing you cannot
# sensibly type.
Buttons\Placing.lua
Buttons\Feature.lua

UnitFrames\Plates.lua

# The cast row one enemy bar carries, before the file that builds the bar. Its
# own file rather than forty more lines in EnemyBars.lua, and a real seam: it
# knows nothing about a plate, a list or a widget pool, and everything in it
# takes a widget and draws on it.
UnitFrames\Cast.lua

# Your own cast bar, after the file it takes every answer about a cast from and
# before nothing in particular: it draws on a frame of its own and no other file
# in this folder knows it exists. Cast.lua says whether there is a cast, how far
# along it is and what the seconds read; this places a bar under the swing timer
# and draws that on it, plus the one state a mob's cast bar has no use for,
# which is the cast that was interrupted.
UnitFrames\PlayerCast.lua

UnitFrames\EnemyBars.lua

# The target's own buff and debuff rows, before the file that builds the block
# they hang under. Its own file for the reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows
# nothing about a portrait, a gauge or an Edit Mode anchor, and everything in it
# takes a skinned frame and draws two rows under it. It also hides the client's
# row, which is the half that cannot be a detail: leaving both up is two answers
# for where an aura goes.
UnitFrames\Auras.lua


# The player, target and target of target frames, in three files. Skin.lua is
# the part: it builds the three secure buttons, decides whether each one is
# wanted, shows and hides them in the order that survives combat, and places
# them. The two below it are the two subjects it used to hold, and each is
# loaded before the file that calls it.
#
# The block itself: the square, the two bars, the four strings, the badges, and
# where the three blocks hang off each other. After Auras.lua, whose rows it
# builds and lays out.
UnitFrames\Block.lua

# One pass over one block: the colours off the unit, the two bars, the four
# numbers, the incoming heal, the portrait and the badges. Its own file for the
# reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows nothing about an anchor or a drag,
# and everything in it takes a block that already exists and writes what
# changed.
UnitFrames\Paint.lua

UnitFrames\Skin.lua

# One party or raid member's block: build it, lay it out, tick it. Its own file
# for the reason UnitFrames\Cast.lua is: it knows nothing about a header, an
# order or a setting other than the two sizes handed to it, and everything in it
# takes one button and draws on it. Before Group.lua, which is the only caller.
UnitFrames\Member.lua

# The party and raid header: its attributes, the slot order and where the block
# of frames sits. These are our frames rather than Blizzard's wearing our skin,
# because PartyMemberFrame1 is bound to party1 in XML and there is no supported
# way to point it at anyone else, so the moment the order is decided by role the
# client's frames cannot draw it. SecureGroupHeaderTemplate is the answer the
# client already ships: it makes one secure unit button per member and shows and
# hides them itself as the roster changes.
#
# After Unit\Role.lua, which decides the band each member sorts into, and after
# UnitFrames\Member.lua, which draws one of them. Both are far above this line.
UnitFrames\Group.lua

# The part's page in the options window, before Feature.lua, which is the only
# file that hands it to Core. Split out of Feature.lua at the 800 line gate,
# along the seam that file already had: everything in here reads a setting,
# draws a control and writes it back, and nothing in here decides anything.
UnitFrames\Panel.lua

UnitFrames\Feature.lua

# The meters. A damage and healing readout and a threat readout, two panes in
# one frame with no window round them. Two files that used to be here are under
# Unit\ now, both because a second part wanted the same answer: who is in the
# group is Unit\Roster.lua, because the enemy bars were walking the raid on
# their own ticker to get it, and which tree somebody took is Unit\Spec.lua,
# because the party frames sort by the role that tree makes them. Meter and
# Threat are independent of each other: one reads the combat log and one samples
# the client's threat API, and neither knows the other exists. Feature.lua last,
# as everywhere else.
Meter\Meter.lua
Meter\Threat.lua
Meter\Window.lua

# Where you stand on the mob under the cursor, on every tooltip in the addon
# rather than in the threat pane alone. One source registered into ns.Tip
# against the unit kind: the percentage on a client that keeps one, and who the
# mob is swinging at on a client that does not. After Unit\Threat.lua, which it
# asks, and in this folder rather than beside the part that opens the box,
# because the part that opens it knows nothing about threat and this knows
# nothing about hovering. Feeds\Worth.lua is the same shape one kind over.
Meter\Standing.lua
Meter\Feature.lua

# The swing timer. Swing.lua is the clock, out of the combat log and
# UnitAttackSpeed; Slam.lua is where the press that costs no swing lands;
# Gauges.lua draws both hands and the band; Feature.lua last as everywhere
# else. Slam loads after Swing because a finished Slam restarts the swing and
# calls into it, and Gauges after both because it draws what the two answer.
# After the meters and before the art for no reason but the panel's order.
Swing\Swing.lua
Swing\Slam.lua
Swing\Gauges.lua
Swing\Feature.lua

# The buff nag. Upkeep.lua is what should be up and is not, out of your own
# auras and out of GetWeaponEnchantInfo; Racials.lua is the cooldown you own and
# have not pressed; Nag.lua draws the row; Feature.lua last as everywhere else.
# Racials loads after Upkeep for no reason but reading order, because neither
# knows the other exists and only Nag.lua reads both. After the swing timer
# because it is the other thing on screen that says what to press next, and it
# needs UI\Ability.lua, which is far above this line.
Buffs\Upkeep.lua
Buffs\Racials.lua
Buffs\Nag.lua
Buffs\Panel.lua
Buffs\Feature.lua

# The cooldowns, in two layers. Cooldowns.lua is what is on the row, out of the
# two lists your spec registered and the two trinket slots; Row.lua draws the
# rotation ones as big squares along the top and the long ones smaller and
# docked under them; Panel.lua is the same row on the options page, where you
# drag the squares into the order you want; Feature.lua last as everywhere else.
# After the buff nag because it is the same question one step out, and it
# needs UI\Ability.lua, which is far above this line.
Cooldowns\Cooldowns.lua
Cooldowns\Row.lua
Cooldowns\Panel.lua
Cooldowns\Feature.lua

# The row of what you have out: a shaman's four totem slots today, a warrior's
# three stances the day somebody writes them down. Standing.lua is the slots and
# what is in each one, Row.lua draws them, and Feature.lua last as everywhere
# else. After the cooldown row because it is the third of the rows over your
# character that answer what you have got, and it needs UI\Aura.lua rather than
# UI\Ability.lua: a totem's sweep fills as it runs out where a press empties as
# it comes back. Both are far above this line.
Standing\Standing.lua
Standing\Row.lua
Standing\Feature.lua

# The feeds. Two columns of what just happened: what dropped, and what landed on
# you. Stream.lua is the frame one of them sits in, its anchor and its settings,
# and it loads first because the other two ask it for one as they load. Loot and
# Combat share nothing with each other: one parses the client's loot sentences
# and one reads the combat log, and neither knows the other exists. Feature.lua
# last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Buffs because it is the last of the parts that draw over the world, and
# after Unit\Roster.lua, which is what the combat feed asks whether a GUID is
# one of yours. Both are far above this line.
#
# Purse.lua between the frame and the two feeds. It is the ledger behind the
# status strip along the bottom of the loot feed: what this character carries,
# what the account carries, and how fast that is moving. Before Loot.lua because
# that file folds the purse's defaults into its own as it loads.
Feeds\Stream.lua
Feeds\Purse.lua
# Auction.lua asks whichever auction addon the player has installed what an
# item goes for, and answers nothing at all where they have none. Nothing in
# the addon requires it and no TOC names one as a dependency: it is one line of
# one tooltip, and a player with no scanner is told nothing about that.
#
# Before Loot.lua because the loot feed's tooltip is the only thing that asks.
Feeds\Auction.lua

# What an item is worth, on every tooltip in the addon rather than on loot rows
# alone. One source registered into ns.Tip against the item kind: the vendor
# price, the stack that price comes to, and whatever Auction.lua's scanner says
# it goes for. After Auction.lua, which it asks, and before Loot.lua, which
# hands it the price it recorded when the item dropped.
Feeds\Worth.lua
Feeds\Loot.lua
Feeds\Combat.lua
# What you just picked up, floated across the screen. The second reader of
# ns.LootLine after Feeds\Loot.lua, answering to its own switch because a drop
# the feed's quality chips filtered out is still a drop you want to see. After
# Ck\Float.lua, which is the column it pushes into.
Feeds\Floats.lua
Feeds\Feature.lua

# The breakdown. What this character does, counted out of the combat log and
# kept between sessions: one row per ability, how often it lands, how often it
# crits, what stopped it and how much of your damage it is. Breakdown.lua counts
# and Window.lua draws the table into a window of its own, so Window loads after
# it, and Feature.lua last as everywhere else.
#
# It is not the meters and shares nothing with them. Meter/Meter.lua totals the
# pull you are in and forgets it when the next one starts; this never forgets
# and never reports a rate. Both read the same event and each registers its own
# handler, which is the house pattern: Swing, Reaction and the combat feed all
# do the same.
#
# After the feeds because it is the last thing in the addon that reads the
# combat log, and after Core\Panel.lua and the widget kit, which are far above
# this line, because the window is built out of the second and opened from the
# first.
Breakdown\Breakdown.lua
Breakdown\Window.lua
Breakdown\Feature.lua

Artwork\Artwork.lua
Artwork\Feature.lua

# The experience and reputation rails. Progress.lua is every call this part
# makes to the client, including the session's own experience clock, which no
# API answers; Rails.lua draws the two bars; Feature.lua last as everywhere
# else. Directly after Artwork because that file leaves MainMenuExpBar and
# StatusTrackingBarManager alone on purpose, and this is the part that replaces
# them. It needs UI\Gauge.lua and Unit\Color.lua, both far above this line.
Progress\Progress.lua
Progress\Rails.lua
Progress\Feature.lua

# The minimap. Shape.lua makes the frame square and as wide as you asked for,
# Corral.lua holds the other addons' buttons behind one square, and Feature.lua
# last as everywhere else. Shape first because the corral's default position is
# a corner of the map and the map has to have been resized before that corner
# means anything. Next to Artwork because it is the same job on another of
# Blizzard's frames: furniture off, everything reversible in one call.
Minimap\Shape.lua
Minimap\Clock.lua
Minimap\Corral.lua
Minimap\Feature.lua

# The chores. Nothing here is on the combat path, and the first five draw
# nothing at all, so they are independent of each other and of load order within
# the part. Clutter decides what is finished with and Destroy is the window that
# acts on it, so Clutter loads first. Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# Vendor and Repair both wake on MERCHANT_SHOW and share nothing else: one
# empties the bags and one pays for the mending, and neither knows the other
# exists. Vendor is listed first because it is the older of the two and for no
# other reason.

# What is worth picking up, which is a question Comfort\Loot.lua asks and does
# not answer. Above it because that is the order the two read in and for no
# other reason: nothing is resolved at load, the filter is asked one slot at a
# time over a corpse, and a character who has turned none of it on loots the way
# the addon looted before this file existed.
Comfort\Wanted.lua
Comfort\Loot.lua

# Destroying what the loot filter refused, for the person who skins. A corpse
# only opens for a skinner once every slot is gone, so with that switch on a
# slot the filter would have left is looted anyway and the item is destroyed
# when it lands. Under Comfort\Loot.lua because that is the only thing that
# calls it. Neither reads the other while it loads, so the order here is for
# whoever is reading rather than for the client.
Comfort\Leftovers.lua
Comfort\Vendor.lua
Comfort\Repair.lua
Comfort\Camera.lua

# Thanking a stranger who buffs you. A chore in the sense the four above are:
# something you would otherwise do by hand and usually do not, because by the
# time your hands are free the name has scrolled away. It reads the combat log
# for an aura a player you are not grouped with put on you and whispers them
# back. After Unit\Roster.lua, far above this line, which is what it asks
# whether a caster is one of yours.
Comfort\Thanks.lua

# The fanfare when you level: five seconds of an eighties chorus over the
# client's own chime, out of BestAround, which is the addon everybody who has
# heard this joke heard it from. Not a chore, and the only thing in the part
# that saves you nothing at all. It is here because Comfort owns the settings
# that belong to no feature, and because a tab is a cheaper home for one tick
# box than a rail entry. Depends on nothing: one event, one file in Media, and
# it goes quiet on a client that will not take the call. The file is the one
# thing this addon names and does not ship, because the recording is not ours;
# without it the part is silent and says so.
Comfort\Fanfare.lua

# The error filter. Not a chore like the four above it, in that nothing runs
# for you; it is a list of messages you have decided you do not need, and one
# replaced method standing in front of the frame that would draw them. Here
# because Comfort is the part that owns the settings that belong to no feature,
# and because the list is one tab rather than a rail entry of its own.
Comfort\Errors.lua

# The reagent list. Not a chore either, and it has no switch of its own: it is
# a page of notes the loot filter reads. Every time a profession window opens it
# writes down every item every recipe you know wants, under the name of the
# profession that wanted it, so that a corpse can be asked whether a slot is
# something you craft with. It scans whether or not the filter is on, because a
# list that only filled while the setting was on would be empty for whoever
# turns the setting on. Depends on the trade skill and craft shims in
# Core\Core.lua and on nothing else in the part.
Comfort\Reagents.lua

Comfort\Clutter.lua
Comfort\Destroy.lua
Comfort\Feature.lua

# The social part. Who you play with, what they said and how you talk to them.
# People.lua holds the groups and Rooms.lua reads them to work out which
# conversation a line belongs to, so it loads before Feed.lua, which routes
# through it. History.lua is what survives a logout, and it is after Rooms.lua
# because which lines those are is Rooms.lua's answer rather than its own.
# Compose.lua is the line you type and knows only about channels.
# Blizzard.lua takes the client's own chat window off the screen and hands what
# it would have drawn to the feed. Voice.lua shares nothing with any of them: it
# is one setting and a channel to join. Window.lua draws what the other five
# produce, and Feature.lua is last, as everywhere else.
Chat\People.lua
Chat\Rooms.lua
Chat\History.lua
Chat\Compose.lua
Chat\Field.lua
Chat\Feed.lua
Chat\Blizzard.lua
Chat\Voice.lua
Chat\Copy.lua
Chat\Window.lua
Chat\Feature.lua

# The mail window. The addon's own, at the mailbox, in place of the client's.
#
# Who.lua is the one question the part is built around, which is what a
# recipient is: a character of yours, somebody you know, or a name nothing has
# ever seen. It loads first because everything else colours by its answer, and
# it holds no list of its own for two of the three: Feature.lua registers the
# purse ledger and the chat part's groups as tests, because both lists already
# exist and a copy is a copy to keep in step.
#
# Draft.lua is what you are writing and how many mails it divides into, Send.lua
# is the conversation with the server that walks those mails one at a time, and
# Inbox.lua is what is waiting and the sweep that takes it. None of the three
# knows the other two exist; Window.lua draws all of them, and Blizzard.lua
# moves the client's own window out from under it. Bags.lua takes the client's
# own bag click over while that window is open, so a right click puts a stack on
# the letter rather than eating it. Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# After the Chat part, because Feature.lua reads Chat/People.lua's groups at
# load, and after Feeds, because it reads Feeds/Purse.lua's ledger the same way.
# Both are far above this line.
Mail\Who.lua
Mail\Draft.lua
Mail\Send.lua
Mail\Inbox.lua
Mail\Blizzard.lua
Mail\Bags.lua
Mail\Window.lua
Mail\Feature.lua

# The quest log. The addon's own, in place of the client's six-line strip.
#
# Client.lua is the one file in the folder that touches a client API, and it
# loads first because everything else reads through it. It carries the two rules
# the quest log has and nothing else in the addon does: every header must be
# open before an index means anything, and the selection cursor those calls read
# is shared with every other addon and has to be put back where it was found.
#
# Party.lua is who else in your group is on a quest, which the client answers on
# some builds and Questie answers for anyone running it, and it loads before
# Log.lua because the log reads it for every row.
#
# Log.lua turns the client's flat run of rows into zones holding quests, which
# is the shape the left column draws. Where.lua is the optional half: Questie
# knows who takes a quest back, how far the nearest thing you still have to kill
# is, and every place on the map the quest has anything at, and every answer it
# gives degrades to nil rather than to an error. The picture those places are
# drawn on is UI\Chart.lua, far above this line: it takes a map id and a list of
# points and knows nothing about quests, which is why it is in the kit and not
# here. Window.lua draws the three columns, Blizzard.lua puts the client's own
# log in the attic and redirects the L key, Tracker.lua points Questie's tracker
# at this window under the same switch, TrackerOff.lua switches that tracker off
# through Questie's own call under a switch of its own, and Feature.lua last, as
# everywhere else.
#
# Column.lua is the tracker itself, and it is a different thing from the file
# beside it: Tracker.lua is about somebody else's tracker, and this is a column
# of the quests you are standing in, drawn over the world under the same switch
# that takes Questie's off the screen. Its quests are Log.lua's zones and
# nothing else, and it is after Window.lua because a click on one of its rows
# opens that window through Tracker.lua's swap.
#
# After Core\Attic.lua, which is where Blizzard's window goes, and after the UI
# layer, which is what the window is built out of. Both are far above this line.
Quests\Client.lua
Quests\Party.lua
Quests\Log.lua
Quests\Where.lua
Quests\Drops.lua
Quests\Window.lua
Quests\Blizzard.lua
Quests\Tracker.lua
Quests\TrackerOff.lua
Quests\Column.lua
Quests\Feature.lua

# Why an item matters to you: an objective in your log it feeds, a reagent a
# profession of yours still gains from, or something the loot filter would have
# left on the corpse. One answer in a fixed order, so two callers cannot rank
# the same item differently.
#
# A tree of its own rather than a file in Core, which is where it was written.
# It reads Quests\Client.lua, Comfort\Reagents.lua and Comfort\Wanted.lua, and a
# base that every part names may not depend on three parts: every window that
# wants a colour would depend on the quest log through the floor.
# scripts/trees.lua refuses that outright and it is the one rule there with no
# allow-list. After all three of the parts it reads, and before the windows
# that ask.
Need\Need.lua

# The character sheet.
#
# Worn.lua is what you have on and the two calls that change it, Compare.lua is
# shift held over a piece of gear anywhere in the addon, Stats.lua works
# out what the client will not say, which is how often you miss, Skills.lua and
# Reputation.lua read the two lists the client's own sheet keeps behind tabs,
# Readout.lua is the column of headed rows those three are drawn in, Paperdoll.lua
# is the gear page and hosts the stats column down the right of it, Window.lua is
# the three tabs over one window, Blizzard.lua puts the client's own sheet in the
# attic and takes the C key, and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Core\BlizzHide.lua, which owns the switch list Blizzard.lua signs
# into and which is above this line. Core\Attic.lua, where the client's sheet
# goes, is far above it.
Character\Worn.lua
Character\Compare.lua
Character\Stats.lua
Character\Skills.lua
Character\Reputation.lua
Character\Readout.lua
Character\Trace.lua
Character\Paperdoll.lua
Character\Window.lua
Character\RepWindow.lua
Character\Blizzard.lua
Character\Feature.lua

# The talent window. All three trees side by side, every talent on the screen
# at once, in place of the client's one-tree-at-a-time frame.
#
# Read.lua is the one file in the folder that touches a client API, and it
# loads first because everything else reads through it: three shapes of the
# same answer across the two clients, and the talent group carried down every
# call whether or not this client has a second one. Cost.lua is what unlearning
# costs, off the one moment the client says it, which is its own dialog at the
# trainer. Board.lua draws one tree, Window.lua puts three of them side by side
# with the spec strip over them and the two numbers along the foot, Blizzard.lua
# keeps the client's own frame from loading and takes the N key, and
# Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Character, whose Blizzard.lua this one is shaped like, and after
# Core\BlizzHide.lua, which owns the switch list Blizzard.lua signs into.
# UI\Slot.lua, whose square every talent is drawn on, and UI\Scan.lua, which
# reads the client's own description of one, are far above this line.
Talents\Read.lua
Talents\Cost.lua
Talents\Board.lua
Talents\Window.lua
Talents\Blizzard.lua
Talents\Feature.lua

# The spell book. One row per spell with every rank you know folded behind a
# button beside it, in place of the client's one-entry-per-rank pages.
#
# Read.lua is the one file in the folder that touches a client API: it walks
# the tabs, drops the ranks the trainer has not sold you and groups what is
# left by name. Window.lua draws the rows on secure squares, so a click casts
# and a drag lands on a bar, and holds the pick per character; Blizzard.lua
# puts the client's frame in the attic and takes the P key; Feature.lua last,
# as everywhere else.
#
# After Talents, whose Blizzard.lua this one is shaped like, and after
# Core\BlizzHide.lua, which owns the switch list Blizzard.lua signs into.
# UI\Slot.lua, whose square every row is drawn on, and UI\Widgets.lua, whose
# dropdown the fold-out opens, are far above this line.
Spellbook\Read.lua
Spellbook\Window.lua
Spellbook\Blizzard.lua
Spellbook\Feature.lua

# The world hover. A creature under the cursor, described in the addon's own box
# instead of Blizzard's.
#
# It is the one hover no frame in this addon owns. Everything else is answered
# by an OnEnter on something the addon drew; the cursor over a mob is on
# WorldFrame, and the client resolves the unit, fills GameTooltip and shows it
# with no script in the sequence for an addon to write. So this part hears
# UPDATE_MOUSEOVER_UNIT, opens ns.Tip on the cursor, and asks UI\Scan.lua to
# hold the client's own box down while ours is up.
#
# Last of the parts that draw over the world, and after Meter, whose threat line
# is the one thing besides the client that has anything to say about a mob.
# Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
World\World.lua
World\Feature.lua

# The dungeon log. Every boss in the game down the left, the client's own map of
# the dungeon in the middle with the bosses marked on it, and what the one you
# are reading drops on the right. There is no adventure guide on either of these
# clients, so nothing here replaces a window and nothing takes a key: it asks
# for Shift-L and holds it as an override.
#
# Book.lua owns the data and loads first, because Baked.lua only assigns into
# the table it declares. Baked.lua is generated by scripts/bake-dungeons.sh out
# of Questie's own databases: the editorial half, which dungeons there are and
# what order their bosses are fought in, is written by hand in that script, and
# every number in the file is read out of Questie rather than typed. A boss name
# in the hand-written list that Questie cannot place stops the bake.
#
# Places.lua is which dungeon maps this client has, walked out of C_Map's own
# tree the way Map/Zones.lua walks it for zones. Loot.lua turns a baked item id
# into what the client says about it and drops any row the client disagrees
# with, which is the guard against a wrong id drawing a real but wrong sword.
# Seen.lua is the ledger: nothing on either client says where a boss stands
# inside an instance, so a mark is written the first time you loot it, and any
# drop the bake did not have goes in beside it. Here.lua is which dungeon you
# are standing in, off the map id the client hands back, which is what opens the
# window on the place you are in rather than on the shelf. Window.lua draws the
# three columns, Key.lua holds the key, and Feature.lua last, as everywhere
# else.
#
# After Quests, whose window this one is deliberately shaped like, and after the
# UI layer, which is what it is built out of. UI\Chart.lua, the picture in the
# middle, is far above this line.
Dungeons\Book.lua
Dungeons\Baked.lua
Dungeons\Sheets.lua
Dungeons\Art.lua
Dungeons\Places.lua
Dungeons\Loot.lua
Dungeons\Seen.lua
Dungeons\Here.lua
Dungeons\Shelf.lua
Dungeons\Window.lua
Dungeons\Key.lua
Dungeons\Feature.lua

# The world map. Zones.lua is every zone the client will name, walked out of its
# own map tree, and the one table in the addon the client cannot answer for:
# what level each zone is for. Pins.lua is Questie's markers, read off the
# frames Questie makes for them rather than off the database, because Questie
# has already decided which quests you can take and asking again would get a
# different answer. Mates.lua is everybody else in your group, placed by the
# client itself rather than by a table of zone rectangles. Window.lua draws the
# column and the picture, Blizzard.lua puts the client's own map in the attic
# and takes the M key, and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# Window.lua reads the three above it and none of them knows the others exist,
# so they load first. After Core\Attic.lua, which is where Blizzard's map goes,
# after UI\Chart.lua, which is the picture it draws on, and after Unit\Color.lua,
# which is what a person on the map is coloured by. All three are far above this
# line.
Map\Zones.lua
Map\Pins.lua
Map\Places.lua
Map\Mates.lua
Map\Window.lua
Map\Blizzard.lua
Map\Feature.lua

# The bags. Bags.lua walks the five bags you carry and sorts what is in them
# into piles, on the item class the client already files each one under, and
# nothing in it draws. Stack.lua is the other thing you can ask about the five
# bags you are carrying and it does not draw either: it finds two half stacks of
# one item and drags one onto the other until each item is down to a single
# partial, which is the one rearrangement that only ever frees slots.
# Session.lua is the third file that walks those bags and draws nothing: between
# a press of record and a press of stop it counts what arrives, so a dungeon run
# comes out of the evening as one pile at the top of the window instead of a bag
# you have to remember your way around.
# Grid.lua is the pool of squares, each one built on the client's own bag button
# so the click, the drag and the stack split are the client's code rather than
# ours. Merchant.lua is the row of buttons that is
# only there while a vendor is open, and it does neither job itself: the sale is
# Comfort\Vendor.lua's and the mending is Comfort\Repair.lua's, both of them
# loaded far above this line. Window.lua is the window and the free count along
# the bottom of it, Blizzard.lua takes the nine calls the client opens and shuts
# a bag through so B opens this one, and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Core\BlizzHide.lua, which is where the once-a-second pass
# Blizzard.lua registers on lives, and after UI\Tip.lua, which is the box a
# square opens. Both are far above this line.
Bags\Bags.lua
Bags\Stack.lua
Bags\Session.lua
Bags\Grid.lua
Bags\Merchant.lua
Bags\Window.lua
Bags\Blizzard.lua
Bags\Feature.lua

# The vendor's own rack, in the bag window's shape. Stock.lua walks the merchant
# index and sorts what is on it into the same piles Core\Piles.lua sorts your
# bags into, and nothing in it draws. Buyback.lua is the same shape for the
# other rack, the last twelve things you sold, which the addon has to draw
# because parking the client's frame parks its buyback tab with it.
# Grid.lua is the pool of squares, the bag window's square in the bag window's
# columns, because what you do at a vendor is look for a thing and a picture is
# how you find one; the price, the tokens and how many he has left are in the
# box that opens on it. It draws either rack, because a square knows how to lay
# a thing out and nothing about which of the two it came off. Window.lua is the
# window, the tab across the top that picks which rack is under it, and the two
# numbers along the bottom.
# Blizzard.lua moves the client's own merchant window off the side of the
# screen, which is the one mechanism of the three that works on it: hiding that
# frame and caging it both end the conversation with the vendor. Feature.lua
# last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Bags, whose square this one copies and whose merchant row sells into the
# same session, and after Core\BlizzHide.lua, which is where the
# once-a-second pass Blizzard.lua registers on lives.
Merchant\Stock.lua
Merchant\Buyback.lua
Merchant\Grid.lua
Merchant\Window.lua
Merchant\Blizzard.lua
Merchant\Feature.lua

# The numbers that float off your character. Anchors.lua is the three
# rectangles they come from, which are the only thing here you can see with the
# frames locked; Blizzard.lua puts the client's own damage numbers away while
# these are drawing and gives them back when they are not; Numbers.lua reads the
# combat log and decides colour, size and
# which side; Calls.lua watches the two parts that know when an ability comes
# up and says the word above your head. Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
#
# After Core\CombatLog.lua, whose SHAPES table says where the number is on a
# line, after Unit\Roster.lua for whether a GUID is yours, after Ck\Stream.lua
# which is what carries a number across the screen, and after Buttons so that
# the two readers Calls.lua polls exist before anything asks them.
CombatText\Anchors.lua
CombatText\Blizzard.lua
CombatText\Numbers.lua
CombatText\Calls.lua
CombatText\Feature.lua

# Saved.lua is generated by bake-ui.sh and only assigns a table, so it loads
# after the module that owns the table and before the part that reads it.
EditMode\EditMode.lua
EditMode\Saved.lua
EditMode\Feature.lua

# The addon's own settings, which belong to no feature. Last, because the only
# one of them today is how big a window is drawn and it is applied at
# ADDON_LOADED, by which point every file in this list has run. Behaviour first
# and Feature.lua last, as everywhere else.
Settings\Settings.lua
Settings\Feature.lua
